# riscv.resc -- Renode platform + firmware load for the GD32VF103-style RV32IMAC
# vendor platform in the holoscan_mcu_lite multi-vendor harness.
#
# WHAT THIS IS
#   128 KiB of flash at address 0, 32 KiB of SRAM at 0x20000000, one console
#   UART and the LoadELF of this board's demo image. Deliberately NO network
#   MAC, NO SDRAM and NO interrupt controller: this smoke test sends and
#   receives nothing, no interrupt is ever taken, and the image's tick comes
#   from the machine timer inside the CLINT, which IS modelled (see below).
#
# MEMORY MAP (kept in sync by renode/scripts/selfcheck_vendors.sh against
# firmware/platform/gcc_riscv_hml_mcu.ld, firmware/platform/board_config.h and
# the riscv row of renode/boards/index -- all four must agree or the
# riscv_renode_selfcheck CTest test fails)
#   0x00000000  128 KiB  FLASH   code, rodata, .data initialiser image
#   0x20000000  32 KiB   SRAM    .data, .bss, stack, newlib heap
#   0x40000000           UART0    the only peripheral (THR +0x00, LSR +0x05)
#   0x02000000           CLINT    the machine timer ONLY: mtime low +0xBFF8,
#                                 high +0xBFFC. No PLIC is wired, so no
#                                 platform interrupt has anywhere to go.
#
# RESET IS FIRMWARE, NOT A VECTOR TABLE
#   A RISC-V core has no vector table. Renode's default reset fetch lands at the
#   start of flash (address 0, matching the linker script's FLASH ORIGIN, so a
#   wrong board description would visibly fetch garbage rather than silently
#   run code). startup_riscv.c then programs mtvec to its own trap entry. The
#   `cpu VectorTableOffset 0x00000000` line below exists so selfcheck_vendors.sh
#   has a concrete RISC-V statement to assert against the linker script; it is
#   not a vector table offset in the Cortex-M sense.
#
# THE CPU AND THE CPUs MODEL
#   CPU.RiscV32I is the base 32-bit RISC-V model and cpuType "rv32imac" asks for
#   the multiply/divide and atomic extensions. Renode is not installed anywhere
#   this matrix was written, so BOTH the model name and the cpuType string are
#   transcribed from memory and flagged unverified; a Renode release that spells
#   the core differently (for example CPU.RiscV32IMAC) must be reconciled here
#   and only here, and the comment saying so must not be deleted.
#
# THE TICK
#   The demo's cooperative RTOS shim ticks on the MMIO machine timer, read at
#   +0xBFF8/+0xBFFC of 0x02000000 -- the offsets startup_riscv.c expects. If the
#   model does not clock mtime, the shim's bounded spin budget kicks in and the
#   image prints HML-MCU-DEMO-WARN with the pause fallback; the robot test does
#   not forbid that line, exactly as it does not forbid it on the ARM rows.
#
# ABOUT THE UART MODEL NAME
#   same decision and caveat as the microchip_sam and nxp_lpc rows: no
#   GD32VF103 USART model exists to this harness, so UART.NS16550 is the
#   stand-in and startup_riscv.c writes THR/LSR to match. The two halves agree,
#   which is what lets the image print at all; the INTEGER and the 5-bit
#   LSR/THR block are emphatically NOT the GD32 USART map, and both halves must
#   change together before real silicon is involved.
#
# WHY THE HEAP IS IN SRAM AND SMALLER THAN EVERY OTHER ROW
#   hml_reasm_open(&r, 0) uses the library default frame size of 1 MiB and
#   mallocs TWO buffers, i.e. 2 MiB of heap, which is why the BASE platform
#   (renode/holoscan_mcu_lite.resc) needs its 4 MiB SDRAM window. This board is
#   built with --frame-bytes 4096: 18 KiB of heap inside 32 KiB of SRAM, after a
#   4 KiB stack. Same code paths, same checks, smaller frame cap.
#
# STATUS -- READ BEFORE TRUSTING THIS FILE
#   This platform description HAS NEVER BEEN EXECUTED. The machine this matrix
#   was written on has no Renode, no mono, no dotnet, no riscv32 toolchain, and
#   github.com and downloads.renode.io are unreachable from it. Nothing here has
#   been loaded by a Renode release, and no GD32VF103 data sheet was available.
#   That is exactly why the CTest test that uses it exits 77 (skipped) when the
#   tooling is missing. If the tooling IS present and the platform does not load,
#   run_vendor_smoke.sh returns the emulator's non-zero exit and the test FAILS --
#   loudly, and never as a pass that was not observed.

:name: riscv
:description: riscv vendor platform (GD32VF103-style RV32IMAC, flash @ 0x00000000, UART0 @ 0x40000000, CLINT mtime timer, 128 KiB flash, 32 KiB SRAM, no interrupt controller, no SDRAM). Emulator only; never executed; not silicon.

$elf?=riscv_hml_mcu_demo.elf

mach create "hml_riscv"
machine LoadPlatformDescriptionFromString
"""
cpu: CPU.RiscV32I @ sysbus
    cpuType: "rv32imac"

flash: Memory.MappedMemory @ sysbus 0x00000000
    size: 0x20000

sram: Memory.MappedMemory @ sysbus 0x20000000
    size: 0x8000

uart: UART.NS16550 @ sysbus 0x40000000

clint: IRQControllers.RiscVClint @ sysbus 0x02000000
    -> cpu@0
"""

sysbus LoadELF $elf
# No NVIC and no PLIC: the self-check treats the index row's uart_irq of "-"
# and this file's silence about interrupts as the same fact.
cpu VectorTableOffset 0x00000000

# No showAnalyzer: under renode-test --console the UART is captured by the robot
# file's Create Terminal Tester, and an interactive analyzer window would only
# add a way for the run to fail for a non-code reason.